      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": "We tested the hypothesis that nitric oxide (NO) is involved in vascular nociception of physical stimuli in humans. Vascularly isolated hand vein segments of six healthy volunteers were pretreated with the NO synthase inhibitor, N(G)-nitro-L-arginine methyl ester (L-NAME; 10(-7)-10(-4) M) and repeatedly subjected to noxious thermal (2 degrees C, 52 degrees C) or mechanical stimuli (balloon distention) and, for control, to the endogenous algetic bradykinin (10(-6) M). L-NAME prevented in a concentration-related manner the algesic action of bradykinin, but had no effect on pain evoked by heat, cold, or stretch. NO is therefore not a general chemical link in nociception."},
